1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,560
Look at Orion this winter at the orange

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,700
star on its shoulder. That star is dying.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:09,120
So is Betelgeuse about to explode? And

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,640
what would you see if it did?

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,740
It is called Betelgeuse. One day it will

6
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,460
blow itself apart, and for a few weeks it

7
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:19,960
will shine so brightly that you could see

8
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,400
it in the daytime sky.

9
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:24,460
In this film, I will show you what kind of

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,280
star it is, why it scared astronomers in

11
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:31,920
2019, how a giant star actually dies, what

12
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:33,660
the night it goes would look like from

13
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,520
your garden, and the honest answer to

14
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:40,720
when. Betelgeuse is a red supergiant. It

15
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,600
is enormous. Put it where the sun is, and

16
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:45,740
its surface would reach out past the orbit

17
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:48,580
of Mars and out beyond the asteroid belt.

18
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,420
It is somewhere between five and seven

19
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:53,960
hundred light-years away. Even measuring

20
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:56,640
that is hard because the star is so big

21
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:58,940
and so restless that its center is

22
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,740
difficult to pin down, and it is young.

23
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:04,700
Our sun is four and a half billion years

24
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,760
old and halfway through its life.

25
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,120
Betelgeuse is only about ten million years

26
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,440
old, and it is already near the end of its

27
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:12,840
life.

28
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:16,540
Why so fast? Because it is heavy.

29
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,520
With somewhere around fifteen to twenty

30
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,660
times the sun's mass, it burns its fuel

31
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:25,720
ferociously. It gives out around a hundred

32
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,360
thousand times as much light as the sun.

33
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,920
Big stars live fast and die young.

34
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:35,160
You can find it yourself. On a winter

35
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:37,220
evening, look for the three stars of

36
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:40,040
Orion's Belt, then go up and to the left.

37
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:43,140
Its surface is cooler than the sun's, and

38
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,100
that is why it glows orange instead of

39
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:45,480
white.

40
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:48,660
At the end of 2019, something strange

41
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:51,500
happened. Betelgeuse, normally one of the

42
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:54,200
brightest stars in the sky, began to fade.

43
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,340
By February 2020, it had dropped to about

44
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:59,860
a third of its usual brightness. It was

45
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:01,960
the faintest anyone had measured it in

46
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,680
more than fifty years. The question went

47
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,680
all around the world. Was this it? Was

48
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:09,380
Betelgeuse about to explode?

49
00:02:10,419 --> 00:02:13,040
It was not. Telescopes caught the answer.

50
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:15,780
A huge bubble of gas had burst out from

51
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:18,680
the star's surface and drifted away. As it

52
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:20,740
cooled, it turned into a cloud of dust,

53
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:23,040
and the dust blocked part of the star from

54
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:26,220
our view. A cool patch on the surface made

55
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:27,160
the dimming deeper.

56
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:30,300
Why did it fool everyone? Because from

57
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,680
Earth, all we see is a point of light.

58
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,420
When that point fades, the obvious guess

59
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:37,080
is that something terrible is happening

60
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:40,160
inside. By the spring, Betelgeuse was

61
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,580
back. But the scare taught astronomers

62
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:45,400
something. A dying giant does not fade

63
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:48,320
quietly. It throws off pieces of itself as

64
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:48,980
it goes.

65
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,080
So how does a star like this die?

66
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:56,040
All its life, a star is a balance. Gravity

67
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,600
pulls everything in. The heat of nuclear

68
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,880
fusion in the core pushes everything out.

69
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,100
For most of its life, Betelgeuse fused

70
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:07,220
hydrogen into helium. Now it is fusing

71
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:08,680
helium into carbon.

72
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:11,460
After that will come heavier and heavier

73
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:14,360
elements, each stage burning faster than

74
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,300
the last. The final stages take only

75
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:20,900
years, then days. The end of the line is

76
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,220
iron. Fusing iron does not release energy.

77
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:27,440
It costs energy. So when the core fills

78
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:30,440
with iron, the fire goes out, and gravity

79
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,060
wins.

80
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,640
In less than a second, a core heavier than

81
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,240
the Sun collapses into a ball the size of

82
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:40,720
a city. The outer layers fall in, bounce

83
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:43,320
off it, and are blasted out into space.

84
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:45,580
That is a supernova.

85
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:49,360
What is left behind is a neutron star so

86
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,580
dense that a teaspoon of it would weigh as

87
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,580
much as a mountain. If you saw our film on

88
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,100
neutron stars, you know how strange that

89
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:57,840
place is.

90
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,500
Or if the core is heavy enough, a black

91
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:04,260
hole. We make one of these every week.

92
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,180
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

93
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,960
So imagine the night Betelgeuse explodes.

94
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,380
The first sign would not be light at all.

95
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:17,899
It would be a flood of ghostly particles

96
00:04:18,019 --> 00:04:20,680
called neutrinos streaming out of the

97
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:21,700
collapsing core.

98
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,420
They pass straight through the Earth.

99
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:27,480
Detectors deep underground would catch a

100
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,400
sudden burst, and astronomers would get

101
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:32,400
the alert hours before the star

102
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:33,100
brightened.

103
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:35,720
Then the light arrives.

104
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,720
Within days, Betelgeuse would blaze as

105
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:42,980
bright as the moon at half phase. You

106
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:45,820
could see it in broad daylight. At night,

107
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:48,780
it would outshine everything in the sky

108
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:50,040
except the moon.

109
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:53,660
It would stay that bright for weeks, then

110
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:57,280
fade slowly over months and years until

111
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:59,780
the shoulder of Orion was simply gone.

112
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,820
The hunter would lose a shoulder, and

113
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,260
every person who looked up afterwards

114
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:08,880
would see a different Orion from the one

115
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:09,460
we know.

116
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:11,720
Should anyone worry?

117
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:12,900
No.

118
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:16,900
A supernova only harms life on Earth if it

119
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:18,900
is within a few dozen light-years.

120
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,960
Betelgeuse is far beyond that.

121
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:25,460
We would get the show, not the danger.

122
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,480
And here is a strange thought. The light

123
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:32,200
from Betelgeuse takes centuries to reach

124
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:32,580
us.

125
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,860
If it had exploded sometime in the late

126
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:38,640
Middle Ages, more than five hundred years

127
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:39,000
ago,

128
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:43,180
we would only be finding out about now. So

129
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:44,460
when will it happen?

130
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:47,820
The best estimates say Betelgeuse has

131
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:50,100
around a hundred thousand years left.

132
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,300
That is the answer most astronomers would

133
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:53,760
give you.

134
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:57,620
Not in our lifetimes, almost certainly.

135
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,220
Why can't we just look and tell?

136
00:06:02,644 --> 00:06:04,704
Because the changes that matter happen

137
00:06:04,824 --> 00:06:07,604
deep in the core, and the surface barely

138
00:06:07,704 --> 00:06:09,624
shows them until the very end.

139
00:06:10,644 --> 00:06:12,524
From outside, a star with

140
00:06:13,504 --> 00:06:16,004
years left and a star with a hundred years

141
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:18,304
left can look almost the same.

142
00:06:19,444 --> 00:06:21,524
And the star keeps surprising us.

143
00:06:22,844 --> 00:06:25,044
A few researchers have argued that it

144
00:06:25,084 --> 00:06:27,224
could be much further along than we think

145
00:06:27,684 --> 00:06:29,584
and might go within centuries.

146
00:06:30,444 --> 00:06:32,004
Most do not agree.

147
00:06:33,344 --> 00:06:35,784
The honest answer is that nobody can give

148
00:06:35,824 --> 00:06:38,584
you a date, and in twenty twenty-five,

149
00:06:39,024 --> 00:06:41,684
astronomers reported something new: a

150
00:06:41,784 --> 00:06:43,904
faint glimmer right where a hidden

151
00:06:44,004 --> 00:06:47,044
companion star was predicted to be. A

152
00:06:47,104 --> 00:06:50,104
small star circling Betelgeuse every six

153
00:06:50,204 --> 00:06:52,744
years or so, lost in its glare.

154
00:06:53,964 --> 00:06:57,444
If it is real, it explains a slow pulse in

155
00:06:57,484 --> 00:06:59,624
Betelgeuse's light that has puzzled

156
00:06:59,684 --> 00:07:01,124
astronomers for decades.

157
00:07:02,344 --> 00:07:05,384
The sighting is still tentative. The best

158
00:07:05,424 --> 00:07:07,624
chance to check comes in late twenty

159
00:07:07,724 --> 00:07:10,044
twenty-seven when the two stars are

160
00:07:10,104 --> 00:07:11,084
furthest apart.

161
00:07:12,604 --> 00:07:15,004
We have watched stars explode before.

162
00:07:15,984 --> 00:07:18,784
In the year ten fifty-four, astronomers in

163
00:07:18,824 --> 00:07:22,324
China recorded a new star so bright that

164
00:07:22,344 --> 00:07:24,644
it could be seen in daylight for more than

165
00:07:24,684 --> 00:07:25,484
three weeks.

166
00:07:26,604 --> 00:07:29,724
Today, at that spot in the sky, there is

167
00:07:29,764 --> 00:07:32,304
the Crab Nebula, still expanding.

168
00:07:33,844 --> 00:07:36,304
So is Betelgeuse about to explode?

169
00:07:37,364 --> 00:07:40,124
Not tonight, and probably not for a very

170
00:07:40,224 --> 00:07:44,064
long time, but it will. And when it does,

171
00:07:44,524 --> 00:07:45,824
it will give something back.

172
00:07:46,924 --> 00:07:49,224
Much of the oxygen you breathe and the

173
00:07:49,284 --> 00:07:52,304
calcium in your bones were made inside

174
00:07:52,424 --> 00:07:55,124
giant stars like this one and scattered

175
00:07:55,184 --> 00:07:57,044
across space when they exploded.

176
00:07:58,124 --> 00:08:00,764
Betelgeuse will do the same, seeding the

177
00:08:00,804 --> 00:08:03,664
clouds that will become new stars and new

178
00:08:03,724 --> 00:08:04,264
worlds.

179
00:08:05,844 --> 00:08:08,164
What if the most important thing about a

180
00:08:08,204 --> 00:08:11,703
dying star is not how it ends, but what it

181
00:08:11,764 --> 00:08:12,764
leaves behind?

182
00:08:13,984 --> 00:08:16,364
The bigger question is closer to home.

183
00:08:17,424 --> 00:08:20,544
Long ago, giant stars near a cloud of gas

184
00:08:20,624 --> 00:08:23,604
and dust seeded it with new elements.

185
00:08:24,704 --> 00:08:28,144
That cloud became our sun, our Earth, and

186
00:08:28,364 --> 00:08:28,704
us.

187
00:08:29,884 --> 00:08:32,524
We are made of stars that lived and died

188
00:08:32,684 --> 00:08:34,264
before the Sun was born.
